content.text:
Joaquin, the supervisor is `site supervise --project FA`, a loop running in the background on your laptop. It's part of the `site` tool, set up under "Decisions and work items" in `~/work/CLAUDE.md`. Every 20 minutes it does one pass:
- runs FA's upkeep commands: the CC feeder, the rebalancer, pruning old runs;
- checks each work item's progress;
- watches running jobs and raises events when they end, fail or overrun;
- starts the queued launches.

**Why jobs go through it.** Your goal for this session says "approved runs go through `site launch` (the supervisor fires them)". So when you approve something, I queue it as a launch with its decision and work item, and the supervisor submits it. That design exists so a launch can wait for a condition, such as a copy finishing, and retry when it can't be placed. It also leaves a record of which decision each job ran under.

**The cost,** which you've spotted: a launch can wait up to 20 minutes for the next pass. Today that wasn't the main delay. The held-out launch was queued at 01:15 and fired at 01:18. The two lost hours were the launcher deferring, the bug I fixed. But the wait is real and pointless for a job that is ready now.

**The fix, from now on:** right after queuing a launch, I run one supervisor pass myself (`site supervise --project FA --once`). The job is submitted within a minute and still goes through `site launch`. The background loop can't fire the same launch twice: each launch is claimed before it starts. The 20-minute loop stays only for what has to wait: retries, readiness conditions, and watching running jobs.
